{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786514934,"nonce":"TBWG74V0Jlar4EjVg8NxjGGaqQ8=","title":"**What is wrong.** `TestEnumFilterCeilingsMatchTheirEnum` (`test/contract/dto_schema_test.go:1066-1106`) holds every enum-backed array to `maxItems == len(enum)`, but it only ever looks at query parameters: its walk is `params := ops[opID].queryParams(d)` (`:1073`), and nothing in the file walks `components/schemas`. Six enum-backed arrays live there, across four property names, and every single one of them declares a `maxItems` above its enum. There is no counter-example — the number of enum-backed body arrays that agree with their enum is zero.","message":"**The demonstration.** All six, read out of `api/openapi/openapi.yaml`:\n\n`PolicyDTO.reasons` (`:8083-8089`) is `maxItems: 32`, `uniqueItems: true`, items `$ref NotificationReason`; `NotificationReason` (`:5251-5271`) is a closed enum of **18** values. `CreatePolicyRequest.reasons` (`:10184-10190`) and `UpdatePolicyRequest.reasons` (`:10228-10234`) are the same 32-against-18, with the same `$ref` and the same `uniqueItems: true`. `ChannelTypeDTO.capabilities` (`:7881-7887`) is `maxItems: 16`, `uniqueItems: true`, over an inline enum of **6** (`threading, amend, rich_layout, interactive, broadcast, dedupe_key`). `ChannelTypeDTO.credential_kinds` (`:7874-7879`) is `maxItems: 16` over an inline enum of **6** (`slack_bot_token, slack_app_token, slack_signing_secret, basic, bearer, none`), with no `uniqueItems`. `ChannelTypeDTO.renderers` (`:7891-7895`) is `maxItems: 8`, items `$ref RendererId`, and `RendererId` (`:5380-5383`) is an enum of **3** (`default, slack.default, webhook.json`); no `uniqueItems` there either.\n\n**Why it matters.** Not for the reason the query-parameter gate exists, and the ticket should not pretend otherwise. That gate was built for a ceiling BELOW its enum — its own comment (`:1047-1053`): *\"'give me everything' is the one request every such filter must be able to express, and the caller is refused by its OWN generated client, before a server that would have answered it ever sees the request.\"* All six of these sit above, which refuses nothing. For the four carrying `uniqueItems: true` the extra room is unreachable; for the two that do not, it only permits duplicates. Nothing is broken today. What is at stake is whether the rule the gate encodes is a rule about contracts or a rule about filters, and the answer is not obviously \"contracts\".\n\n`reasons` is the case that argues against a blind extension. CONTEXT.md §5b: *\"A bound (length, count, enum, regex) lives in **three** places — DTO tag, domain constructor, DDL `CHECK` — and they must be **identical**. `TestValidatorMatchesDDL` enforces it.\"* The 32 is that bound, in all three: `internal/notification/api/dto.go:268` (`validate:\"required,min=1,max=32,unique\"`) and `:285` for the update body, `internal/notification/domain/policy.go:32-33` (`MaxPolicyReasons = 32`, commented \"is policies_reasons_ck\"), and `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:122` (`CONSTRAINT policies_reasons_ck CHECK (array_length(reasons, 1) BETWEEN 1 AND 32`). Lowering the contract alone to 18 breaks that identity and fails R9's own gate; lowering all four means a migration to tighten a constraint nothing has ever hit. A query parameter has no such tie — `?type=` is a filter, not a stored column — which is precisely why equality was cheap there and is not cheap here.\n\n`ChannelTypeDTO` is the opposite case and shows the rule is not uniformly expensive either: it is a static, server-authored descriptor (`:7900-7902`, *\"A static provider descriptor\"*) that no client ever sends and no DDL backs, so its three ceilings bind nothing at all and correcting them to 6, 6 and 3 costs a one-line edit each.\n\nSo the finding is a decision that has never been taken: does the equality rule extend to bodies, does it extend only to response-only schemas, or is the real invariant `enum + uniqueItems` — in which case `maxItems` is redundant on the four that carry it and the honest fix is to delete rather than lower it. Whichever answer is right, the current state is the one state that is definitely wrong: six numbers nobody maintains, each of which will keep its value the next time its enum moves.\n\n**Where.** `test/contract/dto_schema_test.go:1066-1106` (the gate and its query-only walk, `:1073`); `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:8083-8089`, `:10184-10190`, `:10228-10234`, `:7874-7879`, `:7881-7887`, `:7891-7895` (the six ceilings), `:5251-5271` (`NotificationReason`, 18), `:5380-5383` (`RendererId`, 3); `CONTEXT.md` §5b bounds rule; `internal/notification/api/dto.go:268,285`, `internal/notification/domain/policy.go:32-33`, `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:122` (the three other places `reasons` 32 is written).\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** The question is answered in writing and CI holds the answer. Either the walk extends to `components/schemas` — with `ChannelTypeDTO`'s three brought to 6, 6 and 3, and `reasons` either changed in all four R9 places (contract, both DTO tags, domain constant, DDL, via a migration) or carrying a named exemption that says the DDL bound is the authority and why — or the gate's doc comment states that body schemas are deliberately out of scope, naming these six as known slack, so the next reader does not have to rediscover them. Whatever the rule turns out to be, no enum-backed array in the contract is left agreeing with its enum only by accident.","files":null}]}